rustc_codegen_js: let `core` supply `eh_personality` in the unwind build mode.

`-Cpanic=unwind` makes rustc require the `eh_personality` lang item in every
crate graph that produces a final artifact, and `std` is the crate that
normally carries it. There is no `std` here. `JSC_PANIC=unwind` applies this
patch (patches/unwind/ is applied after patches/*.patch, and only in that
mode), so an ordinary `#![no_std]` crate builds in the mode with nothing
added. The item is an aborting stub, like `std`'s own one for wasm targets:
the emitted JavaScript catches and rethrows an unwinding exception itself and
never calls a personality routine. `#[cfg(panic = "unwind")]` keeps it out of
an abort build even if the patch were applied there.

--- a/library/core/src/lib.rs
+++ b/library/core/src/lib.rs
@@ -404,3 +404,15 @@
 // the rustdoc documentation for primitive types. Using `include!`
 // because rustdoc only looks for these modules at the crate level.
 include!("primitive_docs.rs");
+
+// rustc_codegen_js: under `-Cpanic=unwind` (the unwind build mode, `JSC_PANIC=unwind`) every final
+// crate needs the `eh_personality` lang item, which `std` normally supplies. There is no `std`
+// here, and an unwind is a JavaScript exception that the emitted code catches and rethrows: no
+// personality routine is ever called. So `core` carries the item, an aborting stub like `std`'s own
+// for wasm targets, and a crate built in that mode needs nothing of its own. Under `-Cpanic=abort`
+// the item does not exist.
+#[cfg(panic = "unwind")]
+#[lang = "eh_personality"]
+fn rust_eh_personality() {
+    crate::intrinsics::abort()
+}
